glib2 and mc: the MMU dependency is about fork(), so say what replaces it

Both packages carry `depends on BR2_USE_MMU`, and both comments give the same
reason: fork(). That is true and it is not the whole story, because neither
package needs fork() to do the thing it is wanted for here.

mc is a file manager with an internal editor and viewer. Built --without-subshell
it browses, copies, views and edits without creating a process at all; the fork
sites are the subshell, the "execute this file" path, and the extfs helpers that
read archives -- and of those, only background file operations genuinely need a
copy of a running mc. The subshell's child execs a shell after nothing but
syscalls; see the note in mc.mk for what actually stands in its way. glib is pulled in as a utility library -- lists, strings, UTF-8 --
and its fork() lives in gspawn.c, which mc only reaches through those same
paths.

So the dependency is removed and the fork sites are dealt with directly:
posix_spawn where the child execs immediately, and a loud failure where it does
not. See br-external/patches/libglib2/ and br-external/patches/mc/.

The `comment` blocks lose their BR2_USE_MMU dependency too. Left in place they
would hide the "needs a toolchain w/ threads, wchar" message on exactly the
configuration that now has to read it.

Note that threads, which sound like the harder requirement, were never a
problem: BR2_PTHREADS_NATIVE is `depends on BR2_USE_MMU || ((BR2_arm ||
BR2_armeb) && BR2_BINFMT_FDPIC)`, and ARM+FDPIC is this board. NPTL without an
MMU is explicitly supported upstream.

diff --git a/package/libglib2/Config.in b/package/libglib2/Config.in
